intro
 Whenever I go on Substack or on blog sites or on forums regarding Sci-fi, I often come across this discourse:
Is the galactic empire realistic? When people ask this question they tend to ask it from the perspective of, is it realistic, scientifically?
Will the laws of physics allow for a galactic empire? Something I'm convinced of, however, is that this is not the question that writers were asking in the past.
It makes me wonder, why is realism of science fiction being framed as scientific plausibility, rather than societal plausibility. This is likely why the most popular sci-fi right now is hard sci fi. Next to it is sci fi that contains current day political messaging, such as encapsulating ideas within the framework of "queerness", 
identity politics, feminism or, what is called "cli-sci": science fiction that focuses on climate change, which I'll be discussing, later on.
 I used to think it was only the result of awards rewarding such topics, but I think it goes deeper than that.
</br>
One major shift in culture, particularly, in regard to education, has been the extent to which humanities education has been deprioritized in favour of STEM education, vocational training, and political criticisms.
In Europe, the European Commission's recent STEM Education Plan is an explicit skills-and-competitiveness initiative.
In the US accountability reforms, such as "No child left behind", have prioritized tested English and Maths.
Evidence shows that, as a result of this initiative, subjects such as social studies, science, art, and music were squeezed. 
Broad research on US arts education links reduced access to the arts with budget constraints as well as accountability pressure.
In England, the English Baccalaureate or EBacc is a school performance measure that tracks pupils taking a core suite of academic GCSEs.
The former EBacc subjects were English, including language and literature, mathematics, Science, particularly: biology, chemistry, physics or computer science, humanities: history or geography, and foreign language.
The current reforms, per 2025-2026, have removed the EBacc performance measure, replacing it with broader reforms with the government changing accountability metrics by proposing to add creative and vocational subjects into the "academic breadth" category, granting them status equivalent to traditional humanities and languages.
</br>
In the Atlantic article, "The Elite College Students who Can't Read Books", Rose Horowitz discusses how an increasing amount of people enter elite Universities unable to read entire book assignments, because they don't know how.
Beyond this, an increasing amount of university course may have students reading criticisms and readings instead of the actual works of literature.
Mark Edumundson wrote an article in the Chronicle of Higher Education against readings. 
In his 2009 article, Edumndson argues:
 that kicking the "addiction" to readings would strengthen the profession, improve teaching, and increase the number of good books of literary criticism.
I watched the Pens and Poison video, "Why English Departments Hate Literature," which Liza Libe gives her experience with the Columbia University English department, transitioning from a high school with a sparse literature program.
What she found was a literature program that didn't require any students to read any actual literure, requiring them to read criticisms instead, particularly Marxist criticism that frames classic literature in current day politics. 
While this is just one person's experience, the fact this is happening in an English department at a major US university is very telling.
The Adam Walker video, "The Decline of English Departments", describes how public Universities have become increasingly interested in STEM fields with humanities programs being not well funded, the increasing price of tuition, as well as the rise of theory.
In addition to the rise of theory, he also site the loss of public influence. 
Thus you have on one side, a public for which short form video and social media is competing with the potential for self education for attention. 
</br>
Thus there are people who are massively ignorant of history and literature along side people who are educated in critical theory wearing the skinsuit of history and literature. 
The trouble is the mentality and reframing of literature does not stay on University campuses, and is very likely why sensitivity readers are placing trigger warnings on old books like Janet and John, and are attempting to rewrite Roald Dahl and Ian Fleming's James Bond books.
This is what happens when you unleash a generation of people who have been indoctrinated to feel contempt for the people, the literature, and the viewpoints of the past.
The way people are being educated in the humanities is worse that merely a lack of education. 
If people are ignorant of history, literature and the arts, they can always learn. 
There are so many resources by which people can learn about history and literature online.
However, if people are taught to have contempt for the white supremacist, colonialist, misogynist slave owners of the past, that represent our own ancestry, 
there is no way that such people can begin to understand foreign cultures with value systems that are foreign to Western sensibilities. 
While I don't believe that people who have responded to indoctrination like this necessarily 
In fact, the problem is so extreme, people who have responded to such kinds of indoctrination can't even begin to understand people from a different political subculture within their own country, much less people from other countries.
