What does all this have to do with 
Where it ties back into the cultural perception of science fiction as well as the kind of fiction that is being produced: 
If you have a society that has been trained to put up a mental wall blocking the people of that society from having any kind of understanding of their own past, to the point that there's no hope of people understanding other cultures or even subcultures, there's little to no hope that many people in this society would be capable of imagining a culture from a source outside planet earth.
Every culture, every philosophy and ideology that exists on earth, can be traced back to previous ideologies on earth. 
The predominant views whose ideas are being pushed upon students are typically critical theory; critical theory can be traced back to Marxism, which can be traced back to Hegel, which can be traced back to Kant, which, ultimately, be traced back to Aristotle.
Another planet would not have any of these people influencing their thinking, and thus would very likely not see reality through a lens of current year politics. 
A disturbing number of people of several generations have been trained to not be able to grasp this concept, or to fathom the mentality of someone who believes a different set of ideas.
When you look at classic sci-fi, there is a visible difference in the level of historical or literary influences on it.
The most prominent example is Foundation, for which Isaac Asimov was influenced by The Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire by Edward Gibbon.
Dune was primarily inspired by The Sabres of Paradise by Lesley Blanch, which was a biography of 19th century Muslim chieftan Imam Shamyl.
The Left Hand of Darkness was inspired by her Taoist beliefs.
Hyperion was inspired by The Canterbury Tales
The Stars My Destination by Alfred Bester was written to be a futuristic version of The Count of Monte Cristo
</br>
No doubt there are many sci-fi books and stories written by people who genuinely love history and literature which still draw inspiration from classic literature, and thanks to discovery portals such as crowdfunding sites, serial fiction websites, and substack, such works are becoming more well known. 
However, among more mainstream works, there seems to be a divide between hard sci-fi and soft-sci fi that draws from humanities based inspiration, and is literary in nature, but is also ideological. Yes there are a few exceptions, but this is what I usually see. 
One of the driving forces behind the ideological push for sci-fi, as well as fantasy has been the shift in culture in the SFWA, drawing away from rewarding merit to a DEI policy the organization seems to be proud of. I'll be talking about that next time.